WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.279
More than 1.5 million people have fled their
homes since war broke out in eastern Ukraine

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:06.850
five years ago.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.670
But on the streets of the capital Kyiv you
would barely notice.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:15.000
In spite of the fighting and a deep economic
downturn, Ukrainian society HAS been able

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.000
to shoulder the burden.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.970
The refugee camps of 2014 have for the most
part been consigned to history.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.650
Most displaced people have found jobs and
somewhere to live.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.300
But as the country elects a new president,
Ukraine’s displaced people are having to

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.779
fight to cast their votes.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:34.290
One of those people is Nelli Selyutina.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:39.040
Five years ago she left her hometown Donetsk
in a hurry, with just a couple of suitcases

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.050
to hand.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.739
I was driving to work with my husband.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:46.329
Suddenly there were armed men blocking the
road.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:48.989
They were really arrogant, pointing their
weapons at anyone who dared to talk back.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:53.149
I told my husband to buy train tickets; we
need to get out of here.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:54.710
It all happened very fast.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.360
I came home from work and we left the same
evening.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:05.220
When I locked the door I already knew that
I would never be coming back.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.530
Nelli and her family left everything behind
- their home, their car and their jobs.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:15.240
Soon after, Donetsk fell under the control
of pro-Russian separatists.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.380
The family found shelter with friends in Kyiv.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.180
But on their first morning in the capital,
their hosts had something important to do

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.760
- vote.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:32.110
A poll that Nelli and her husband were excluded
from, because they were still registered in

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.090
Donetsk.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.810
You see your friends voting but can’t take
part yourself.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.270
If you are able to vote you don’t feel the
need so strongly.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:48.770
But we were in Kyiv, the same people as before,
but without the right to vote.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:54.840
That was really tough for me.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.170
Since then Nelli has made of point of not
letting any elections pass her by.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.280
But it’s not easy.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.630
Each time there’s a vote she has to go and
register from scratch.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.679
Like Nelli, most of Ukraine’s internally
displaced people are still registered in their

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:12.930
homes in what’s now separatist-held territory.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.730
Ukrainian law ties voting rights to citizens’
place of registration.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:21.439
But registering somewhere new is no easy task,
most landlords do not allow their tenants

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:26.650
to register and even if they can it means
losing their refugee status and the benefits

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.400
that go with that.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.430
In the first round of the presidential election
comedian and political newcomer Volodymyr

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.659
Zelensky triggered a political earthquake,
picking up almost double as many votes as

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:38.299
the incumbent Petro Poroshenko.

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.049
It’s a nail-biting contest that Nelli does
not want to miss out on.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:46.510
For the second round Nelli has to go and register
all over again.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.249
In theory that’s only possible after the
results of the first round have been officially

00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:51.349
announced.

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:55.120
But that would leave just a matter of days
to register ahead of the deadline.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.200
Nelli has heard that some election offices
ARE willing to make things easier for internal

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.299
refugees, accepting applications to vote ahead
of schedule.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:05.989
In the queue she meets old friends from Donetsk.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:14.260
They too are desperate to vote.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.069
Having a say about the future of my own country,
that’s really important to me.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.809
We keep coming here and there are just so
many people waiting.

00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:27.950
We keep coming back but the queue doesn’t
get any shorter.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:32.569
Voting is my right but I’m having to fight
for it.

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:40.489
I think they’re doing this on purpose to
stop us voting.

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:41.489
This isn’t a question of negligence.

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:48.889
They could make it all much easier; put more
people on the job.

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:53.010
Here in this Kyiv election office the officials
are not willing to make any exceptions, even

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.589
for refugees.

00:03:54.589 --> 00:03:58.549
Nelli will have to come back again, after
the results of the first round are officially

00:03:58.549 --> 00:03:59.900
announced.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:05.719
One thing is sure - the queues will only get
longer.

00:04:05.719 --> 00:04:09.059
Experts say the current election law is out
of date.

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:13.719
Millions of Ukrainians live far from home,
some of them as refugees, others in search

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.139
of work.

00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:19.979
Linking the right to vote to registration,
a legal formality that’s very hard to obtain,

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:21.530
is simply unfair they argue.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:27.440
Meanwhile, a draft law to make voting easier
is gathering dust in parliament.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.500
The war in Donbass has turned Nelli’s life
upside down.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.840
In Donetsk she managed a chain of sports’
shops.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.140
In Kyiv she now works to help other people
like herself.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.190
Every day her organization receives hundreds
of calls from displaced people.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:47.870
Many of them want to know how to make sure
they can vote.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.759
We had many calls from students.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:55.460
Many of them were registered at their universities
but as soon as they graduate they have a problem.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.240
They can’t register back at home in separatist-held
territory and here on the government-held

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.310
side no-one wants to let them register.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.900
There’s a big group of people who are shut
out of these elections.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.370
These are young, active people - the very
people these elections are held for - but

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.900
they can’t take part.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.110
It’s a problem Nelli knows about first hand.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.889
Her son was just 13 when they left Donetsk.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:19.039
Later this year he’ll reach voting age.

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:22.369
Whether or not he’ll be able to cast a ballot
- his mother can’t be sure.

